---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
---

<BaseLayout
  title="sparsh docs — getting started"
  description="A friendly getting-started guide for adding sparsh to React or plain JavaScript, plus the options you’ll reach for."
>
  <main class="content-page docs-page">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot" /> The docs</p>
      <h1>Okay, how do I<br /><span>add sparsh to my app?</span></h1>
      <p class="lead">Start by letting sparsh watch what happens. Then decide if you want it to step in.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="docs-layout">
      <aside class="docs-sidebar" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#quick-start">Quick start</a>
        <a href="#react-hook">React hook</a>
        <a href="#vanilla">Vanilla JavaScript</a>
        <a href="#options">Options and signals</a>
        <a href="#limitations">Limitations</a>
      </aside>

      <div class="docs-content">
        <section class="docs-section" id="quick-start">
          <p class="section-kicker">01 · REACT</p>
          <h2>Let’s get sparsh watching.</h2>
          <p>Wrap your app (or the part you want to protect) in <code>ActivationGuardProvider</code>. It starts in enforce mode, so sparsh can stop suspicious activations. Choose report mode to observe decisions without blocking.</p>
          <pre><code>{`import { ActivationGuardProvider } from '@sparsh/react'

export function App() {
  return (
    <ActivationGuardProvider
      mode="enforce"
      onDecision={(decision) => {
        if (!decision.allowed) {
          analytics.track('unsteady_activation', decision)
        }
      }}
    >
      <YourApp />
    </ActivationGuardProvider>
  )
}`}</code></pre>
          <p class="doc-note"><strong>Quick heads-up:</strong> React, Vue, DOM, and core are in this repo, but the first public release hasn’t shipped yet. We’ll add the exact install command here when it does.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section" id="react-hook">
          <p class="section-kicker">02 · GIVE A BLOCK A VOICE</p>
          <h2>Don’t leave people wondering.</h2>
          <p><code>useActivationGuard()</code> gives you a ref for a control and an <code>isGuarded</code> flag. Use it to explain a blocked tap and invite the person to try again.</p>
          <pre><code>{`import { useActivationGuard } from '@sparsh/react'

function SaveButton() {
  const { ref, isGuarded } = useActivationGuard()

  return (
    <>
      <button ref={ref} data-guard-key="draft-42">
        Save draft
      </button>
      {isGuarded && <p role="status">This changed. Please try again.</p>}
    </>
  )
}`}</code></pre>
          <p>Got a list that reuses rows? Put the item’s stable ID in <code>data-guard-key</code>. That way sparsh can tell when “row three” now belongs to someone else.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section" id="vanilla">
          <p class="section-kicker">03 · PLAIN JAVASCRIPT</p>
          <h2>No framework? No problem.</h2>
          <p><code>@sparsh/dom</code> has a vanilla <code>createGuard</code> helper. Attach it to the smallest root that contains the interactions you want to watch, then clean it up when that UI goes away.</p>
          <pre><code>{`import { createGuard } from '@sparsh/dom'

const root = document.querySelector('#app')
if (!root) throw new Error('App root not found')

const guard = createGuard(root, {
  mode: 'enforce',
  onDecision: (decision) => console.log(decision),
})

// When the app root is torn down:
guard.destroy()`}</code></pre>
          <p>Using Vue? There’s a Vue 3 binding too: <code>@sparsh/vue</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section" id="options">
          <p class="section-kicker">04 · THE KNOBS YOU’LL REACH FOR</p>
          <h2>What can I tweak?</h2>
          <dl class="option-list">
            <div><dt><code>mode</code></dt><dd><code>enforce</code> or <code>report</code>, for everything or one policy at a time. Defaults to enforce.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>policies</code></dt><dd>Pick from <code>age</code>, <code>continuity</code>, <code>semantics</code>, and <code>doubleFire</code>. They’re all on by default.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>cooldownMs</code></dt><dd>How long a target needs to be around before Age trusts it. Starts at 500 ms.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>onDecision</code></dt><dd>Gets every decision, allowed or blocked, with the policy and reason.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>onSuspect</code></dt><dd>A shortcut for decisions where <code>allowed</code> is false.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>data-guard="off"</code></dt><dd>Let a subtree (like a “Try again” button) opt out.</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="doc-note"><strong>A little tip:</strong> Age is the easiest policy to overtune. If it is too sensitive for your app, set Age to report mode while you tune its cooldown.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="docs-section limitations-section" id="limitations">
          <p class="section-kicker">05 · THINGS TO KNOW</p>
          <h2>What sparsh can’t catch (yet)</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Opacity-only reveals on an element that was already there are invisible to the current age tracker.</li>
            <li>An overlay can cover a button without changing the underlying target; sparsh may not spot that.</li>
            <li>Reused list rows need <code>data-guard-key</code> so sparsh can tell when the item changed.</li>
            <li>Text comparison is opt-in, since counters and “2 minutes ago” labels change all the time.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If sparsh isn’t sure, it lets the interaction through. For the reasoning (and the accessibility bits), see <a href="/how-it-works/">How it works</a>.</p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
